  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A frontal system nearby will maintain rain\nchances through Sunday with below average temperatures.\n\nA stalled front remains across North Carolina this evening with\noverrunning light rain and clouds stretching north to just south\nof I-70/68. Precipitation increases in coverage during the\nsecond half of the night, especially south of I-66 where periods\nof light rain are likely. Some patchy fog is possible, though\nmostly in rural low-lying areas west of the Blue Ridge.\nTemperatures mostly steady in the 50s to around 60F.\n\nA band of light rain, perhaps locally moderate in nature, will \ntrack northeastward across the region through at least the first\nhalf of Sunday. A persistent northeasterly wind should again \nhold temperatures down in the 60s. Any rain chances should \nlargely come to an end just before sundown Sunday. Overnight \nconditions should be fairly similar to tonight with lows again \nin the 50s.\n\nAs a deep upper trough tracks across far eastern Canada, a more\npotent cold front sweeps through the northeastern U.S. on\nMonday. This will favor increasing sunshine, but also a large\nuptick in northwesterly winds. Afternoon gusts up to 20 to 25\nmph are possible, but with highs returning to the upper 60s to\nmid 70s (upper 50s to 60s in the mountains).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Frost and freezing temperatures are possible to\nthe west of the Blue Ridge early next week.\n\nA strong area of high pressure will build over the area next \nweek, leading to an extended period of dry conditions. As a cooler \nairmass works in and high pressure builds overhead, frost and \nfreezing temperatures may be possible to the west of the Blue Ridge \nboth Monday night and Tuesday night. We'll start to see slightly \nwarmer air build in from the south and west later in the week. High \ntemperatures at lower elevations are forecast to be in the 60s \n(upper 50s/low 60s mountains) on Tuesday, upper 60s to low 70s on \nWednesday (60s mountains), and mid 70s (mid-upper 60s mountains) on \nWednesday and Thursday. Low temperatures will gradually moderate \nfrom the 30s/40s early in the week, to the upper 40s/low 50s later \nin the week.",
